  "text": "\n<think>\nHere's a thinking process:\n\n1.  **Analyze User Input:**\n   - **Context:** Tuning an Intel XPU inference server.\n   - **Key Observations (repeated many times):** \n     - Stable baseline decoding\n     - Prompt-sensitive speculative acceptance\n     - Graph capture bucket sensitivity\n     - Need to preserve exact output quality while improving single-request speed\n   - **Question:** Summarize likely bottlenecks and propose an ordered plan.\n   - **Constraints:** \n     - Concise engineering analysis\n     - Concrete next steps\n     - Technical, no marketing language\n     - At least eight dense numbered paragraphs\n     - Ordered plan\n\n2.  **Deconstruct Observations & Map to Bottlenecks:**\n   - *Stable baseline decoding:* Means the core inference loop works, but likely CPU-bound or memory-bound in the standard path. No major crashes, but not optimized for latency.\n   - *Prompt-sensitive speculative acceptance:* Speculative decoding works but varies by prompt. This suggests KV cache management, draft model alignment, or acceptance threshold tuning is suboptimal. Prompt length/content affects memory access patterns or draft model performance.\n   - *Graph capture bucket sensitivity:* Graph compilation (likely oneDNN/Triton/Intel's own graph compiler) is sensitive to input shapes/buckets. This points to inefficient dynamic shape handling, excessive recompilation, or poor bucket sizing leading to suboptimal kernel selection.\n   - *Preserve exact output quality while improving single-request speed:* Strict determinism/quality constraint rules out approximate methods (e.g., quantization without calibration, aggressive pruning, or stochastic sampling changes). Must focus on latency optimization without altering numerical results or sampling logic.\n\n   Likely bottlenecks:\n   - Memory bandwidth/latency (KV cache, weight loading, attention computation)\n   - Graph compilation overhead & dynamic shape handling\n   - Speculative decoding pipeline synchronization & draft model mismatch\n   - CPU-GPU/XPU data transfer & scheduling overhead\n   - Kernel selection inefficiency across prompt lengths\n\n3.  **Develop Ordered Plan (8+ dense paragraphs):**\n   Need to structure as a numbered list of 8+ paragraphs, each dense and technical, covering bottlenecks and concrete next steps in order.\n\n   Paragraph 1: Memory subsystem & KV cache optimization\n   Paragraph 2: Graph capture & bucket management\n   Paragraph 3: Speculative decoding pipeline tuning\n   Paragraph ",
